Step 2 — Grade law: codon grades follow the binomial distribution
Define the degree of a nucleotide as the size of its blade: deg(A)=0,
deg(C)=deg(U)=1, deg(G)=2. The per-nucleotide degree generating polynomial is

1 + 2x + x² = (1+x)².
Degrees add over the three positions, so the codon polynomial is (1+x)⁶:

#{codons of degree k} = C(6,k) = 1, 6, 15, 20, 15, 6, 1.
Equivalently: the 64 codons distribute across the seven grade sectors
exactly as the 64 blades do. The even/odd split is 32/32, and under the
frozen encoding popcount-parity equals pyrimidine-count-parity (64/64).
